@@A@@
===

The integral of equation (1) may also, as is well known, be exhibited in the following form:— \[y = \phi(x - at) + \psi(x + at) \tag{4}\] where \(a^2 = \frac{T}{\mu}\), and \(\phi\), \(\psi\) are arbitrary functions. The function \(\phi(x - at)\) denotes any form of the string@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
 which advances in the direction of positive \(x\) with the ¶ velocity \(a\), but without any other change; and the function \(\psi(x + at)\) denotes a similar form proceeding with the same velocity in the direction of negative \(x\). For any given value of the time \(t\) we@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
 must suppose both functions to be given from \(x = -\infty\) to \(x = +\infty\), and then the motion of the string is determined.
@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===

The determination of the motion of a plucked string will result in this second form of solution, from determining the functions \(\phi\) and \(\psi\), so that
@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===

1) for the values \(x = 0\) and \(x = l\), the value of \(y\) for any value of \(t\) will be constantly \(= 0\). This will be the case, if for any value of \(t\), \[\phi(-at) = -\psi(+at) \tag{4a}\] and \[\phi(l - at) = -\psi(l + at) \tag{4b}\]
@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===

If in the first equation we put \(at = -v\), and in the second \(l + at = -v\), we obtain \[\phi(v) = -\psi(-v)\] ¶ \[\text{and} \quad \phi(2l + v) = -\psi(-v)\] \[\text{so that} \quad \phi(2l + v) = \phi(v) \tag{5}\]
@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===

Hence the function \(\phi\) is periodic, for its value becomes the same when its argument is increased by \(2l\). The same results for \(\psi\).
@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===

2) For \(t = 0\), we must have \(\frac{dy}{dt} = 0\) between the limits \(x = 0\) and \(x = l\). Hence writing \(\psi'(v)\) for \(\frac{d\psi(v)}{dv}\), and putting \(\frac{dy}{dt} = 0\) in equation (4), we obtain \[\phi'(x) = \psi'(x)\]
@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===

And integrating this with respect to \(x\), we have \[\phi(x) = \psi(x) + C\]

¶ Now since neither \(y\) nor \(\frac{dy}{dt}\) are altered by adding the same constant to \(\phi\) and subtracting it from \(\psi\), the constant \(C\) is perfectly arbitrary, and we may@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
 consequently assume it to be \(= 0\), and hence write \[\phi(x) = \psi(x) \tag{5a}\]

3 Since finally at the time \(t = 0\), and within the limits \(x = 0\), \(x = l\), the magnitude \[y, \text{ which is } = \phi(x) + \psi(x) = 2\phi(x),\] must have the value shewn in fig. 18 A@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
 (p. 54a), the ordinates of this figure immediately give the value of \(2\phi(x)\) and of \(2\psi(x)\), by means of equation (5):— \[\begin{aligned} &\text{between } x = 0 \text{ and } x = l \\ &\quad\;\text{,,} \qquad x = 2l \text{ ,, } x = 3l \\ &\quad\;\text{,,} \qquad x = 4l@@A@@
